WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
ESA's juice probe will swing past a dark, untouched, jovian moon in 2031 that may preserve the solar system's original building blocks. This is fascinating frontiers, episode 179. ESA's juice spacecraft will perform a flyby of Jupiter's small moon, calichor, in 2031. The dark, irregular body has received little prior study compared with the Galilean satellites. Scientists hope the encounter will reveal surface compasses

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:42.500
in this position and clues to the moon's capture history. Juice's instruments will collect data during the brief close approach. The observation fits into a broader campaign to understand Jupiter's outer irregular satellites.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:54.080
ESA's juice spacecraft will pass within a few thousand kilometers of calichor, one of Jupiter's irregular satellites that has remained largely unchanged since the solar system's formation.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:12.520
The moon's dark surface and distant orbit suggest it may be a captured object whose composition preserves primordial ices and organics. Juice's suite of cameras, spectrometers, and particle detectors will gather data during the brief encounter, offering the first close look at this class of body.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:21.920
The observation complements the mission's primary focus on the icy Galilean moons while adding context to how Jupiter assembled its retinue of satellites.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:32.700
Scientists will compare the results with ground-based surveys to refine models of outer planet satellite capture. Now we turn to the physics of how smaller objects reach Earth.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:40.220
Researchers have outlined a seven-stage sequence describing how space rocks decelerate and fragment during atmospheric entry.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:51.500
The model incorporates ablation rates, fragmentation thresholds, and terminal velocity calculations. It allows better estimates of ground impact energy and strewn field patterns.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:02:08.240
Validation draws on recovered meteorites and eyewitness reports. The framework is now being tested against recent well-observed fireballs. A typical meteoroid the size of a grapefruit can cross an entire continent in under 10 seconds while traveling at 20 kilometers per second.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:27.520
Friction instantly heats the leading face to thousands of degrees, vaporizing surface layers in a glowing sheath of plasma. Within the next few seconds, the object experiences peak dynamic pressure that can exceed 10 times the force needed to crush granite, triggering fractures that split the body into dozens of fragments.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:44.340
Some pieces ablate completely. Others decelerate enough to drop below supersonic speeds and fall as dark flight. The entire sequence lasts less than half a minute, yet records temperatures, pressures, and chemical reactions impossible to replicate fully in ground laboratories.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:54.660
We can now predict roughly where fragments land, but the precise moment when a given rock will break apart still depends on internal flaws we cannot measure before entry.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:03:03.240
While models help us understand objects arriving from space, European engineers are perfecting how spacecraft return on purpose.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:12.000
The European Space Agency's Draco Reentry Mission has cleared its critical design review, moving the project into detailed manufacturing.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:24.480
Draco will test technologies for controlled atmospheric reentry of spacecraft components. The review confirmed that the missions heat shield, guidance systems, and recovery hardware meet performance targets.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:33.680
Engineers will now integrate flight hardware ahead of a planned demonstration flight. The outcome will inform future European reusable vehicle designs.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:40.800
Reusability goals also depend on ground infrastructure, which brings us to a Scottish spaceport site changing hands.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:53.400
Assets tied to Orbex's planned southern land spaceport have been sold to a Scottish development agency. The transaction keeps the site available for future launch operations despite Orbex's earlier setbacks.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:04:01.680
Local authorities view the move as preserving infrastructure for small satellite missions. No new operator has yet been named for the pad.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.620
The site remains one of the few licensed vertical launch locations in the United Kingdom.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:18.640
While launch sites evolve, a rover on Mars has uncovered unexpected chemistry. The curiosity rover encountered light colored stones in gale crater whose interiors proved to be native sulfur.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:35.100
X-ray spectroscopy confirmed the material after rover wheels cracked several rocks open. Researchers proposed the sulfur formed when buried clathrates decompressed billions of years ago, volcanic or hydrothermal origins appear unlikely at the discovery site.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:45.140
The find adds new detail to models of Mars's ancient sulfur cycle. Mars continues to surprise us. Back on Earth, another glacier is quietly shrinking.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:51.980
The pastors, a glacier in Austria, retains one narrow icefall that still connects its upper and lower sections.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:05:01.400
Satellite and ground measurements show the feature is narrowing each year. If the icefall severs, the lower glacier will become isolated and melt faster.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:10.440
Researchers continue monitoring the site as a bell weather for Alpine ice loss. The glacier remains the country's largest despite decades of shrinkage.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:17.680
Earth observations also benefit from new satellite constellations. Next, we look at Europe's latest weather spacecraft.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:25.080
A detailed look inside the Kuru launch complex shows how the MTG-I-2 satellite is being ready for flight.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:32.780
Technicians are performing final checks on instruments designed to monitor European skies and support disaster early warning systems.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:41.980
The facilities clean rooms and fueling stations handle the spacecraft's final integration. The mission will join Europe's third generation weather constellation.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:51.860
Ground teams continue rehearsals ahead of the scheduled liftoff. Operational satellites keep flying. A university team in Australia is preparing three new ones.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:59.660
The BINAR-567 mission, consisting of three CubeSats, is slated for launch in the fourth quarter of 2026.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:05.740
The spacecraft will conduct technology demonstrations and educational outreach from low Earth orbit.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:13.900
Project teams at Curtin University have completed integration and environmental testing. The launch provider has not yet been announced.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:24.800
The mission continues Australia's growing small satellite program. Small satellite programs are expanding. Researchers are also expanding how we read the sky's long-term memory.

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:33.360
The square-kilometreq array is being used as a case study for connecting historical astronomical archives with contemporary observations.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:45.120
Project scientists are digitizing centuries-old sky records to compare with new radio surveys. The effort highlights how cultural memory of the sky can inform current research questions.

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:56.380
Workshops have brought together historians, indigenous knowledge holders, and astronomers. Early results show patterns in past comet and Aurora reports that align with modern catalogs.

00:06:56.920 --> 00:07:02.260
Looking backward in time leads naturally to looking for even older cosmic events preserved on the moon.

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:15.920
Meteoroid strikes on the lunar surface can unearth material that recorded past supernova events. University of Hawaii scientists are developing methods to extract and date these cosmic ray signatures from regolith samples.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:25.000
The approach treats the moon as a long-term archive of galactic events. Future sample return missions could supply the material needed for analysis.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:32.020
While the moon preserves ancient records, a new test facility on earth is helping future spacecraft slow down.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:41.040
Tokyo metropolitan university researchers have built a facility that fires miniature vehicles through shockwaves while applying strong magnetic fields.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:46.040
The setup reached magnetic intensities higher than earlier permanent magnet experiments.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:52.360
Results will guide development of magneto hydrodynamic arrow-breaking systems for reusable spacecraft.

00:07:52.820 --> 00:08:00.560
The platform operates at speeds above seven kilometers per second. Future tests aim to scale the technology toward flight hardware.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:14.080
Reentry technology keeps improving. Classification debates about distant worlds continue. A new article questions whether Pluto's 2006 reclassification as a dwarf planet was scientifically justified.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:27.560
The piece examines the international astronomical union's definition and its application to distant icy bodies. Critics argue the criteria overlook dynamical similarities between Pluto and the major planets.

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:38.680
Supporters maintain the distinction helps categorize the growing population of trans-Neptunian objects. The discussion continues to influence how textbooks present solar system taxonomy.

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:45.360
Taxonomy questions remind us of shifting boundaries. Education partnerships are also crossing borders.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:56.660
Former NASA astronaut Donald Thomas dedicated the IEM Innovation Park in Kolkata. The facility will host education programs and technology demonstrations aimed at students.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:06.400
Thomas highlighted parallels between the park's goals and NASA's outreach efforts. Local organizers expect the site to support aerospace and robotics projects.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:16.900
The event marks another step in expanding international space education partnerships. Education efforts continue while policy discussions explore new training structures.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:24.040
A new analysis examines the implications of establishing a dedicated space academy for commissioning military officers.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:33.040
The concept would create a specialized pipeline separate from existing service academies. Proponents site growing demand for space domain expertise.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:43.060
Skeptics question whether a new institution is necessary given current training programs. The discussion centers on curriculum focus and long term for structure needs.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:53.040
Institutional questions persist. Launch reliability remains an ongoing challenge. Another Chinese orbital launch attempt ended in an explosion shortly after liftoff.

00:09:54.080 --> 00:10:01.380
Details on the vehicle and payload remain limited in public reporting. The incident follows a string of similar failures in the past year.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:10.680
Investigators are examining telemetry for clues to the cause. Keep an eye on the next steps for European reusable reentry systems after the Draco review.

